---
// pitomd.com/guides/igdb — connect IGDB (via a Twitch developer app) to
// Pito Studio's Games Library. Linked verbatim from Studio's own setup
// wizard as "pitomd.com/guides/igdb"
// (internal/ui/studio_cockpit_wizard_render.go's gamesCredsBox).
//
// Written for a non-technical reader (owner: "the canonical reader is my
// wife") — the "How it's handled" section leads with a plain trust
// statement, not the config file; config.toml gets one aside at the very
// end, never the opening clause.
//
// SOURCE OF TRUTH for the credential flow: pito-tui's README.md
// ("Configure IGDB credentials") — client ID shown plain, client secret
// masked to its last six characters, written straight into the `[igdb]`
// table of `~/.config/pito/config.toml`, live immediately, no restart.

import GuideLayout from "../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro";

const TITLE = "Connect IGDB — Pito Studio guides";
const DESCRIPTION =
  "How to create free Twitch developer credentials and connect IGDB to Pito Studio's Games Library — what you need, why it's useful, and how it's kept private.";

const pageUrl = new URL("/guides/igdb/", Astro.site).href;
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "HowTo",
      name: TITLE,
      url: pageUrl,
      description: DESCRIPTION,
    },
  ],
};
---

<GuideLayout
  title={TITLE}
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  eyebrow="Games Library · IGDB"
  heading="Connect IGDB."
  dek="IGDB is where Studio's Games Library gets its cover art, release dates, scores and play times. It signs you in through Twitch, so you'll set up a small, free Twitch app and copy two codes into Studio."
  slug="igdb"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section id="need">
    <h2>What you need</h2>
    <ul class="guide-checklist">
      <li>
        <strong>A Twitch account</strong> — any account works, even one you've never
        streamed from.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Two-factor authentication</strong> turned on for that account — that's
        the extra step where Twitch texts or emails you a code when you sign in. Twitch
        won't let you set up a developer app without it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>About five minutes.</strong> You'll only need to do this once.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="why">
    <h2>Why Studio wants this</h2>
    <p>
      Add a game by hand and you get a title and not much else. Connect IGDB and
      every game fills itself in the moment you search for it — cover art,
      platforms, genres, release date, even a score.
    </p>
    <p>
      You don't have to do this. Skip it and Studio still works fine — you'll
      just be typing in game details yourself instead of Studio finding them for
      you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="handled">
    <h2>How it's handled</h2>
    <div class="guide-callout">
      <p>
        The two codes you paste in — Studio calls them a Client ID and a Client
        Secret — stay right here on this computer. Studio uses them to talk to
        IGDB directly; nothing passes through a Pito server, because there isn't
        one.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Studio's own settings, the first code is shown to you in full (it
        just names an app, so it isn't private) and the second stays mostly
        hidden, the same way Studio hides a licence key. Want to disconnect?
        Delete the app from the Twitch Developer Console and Studio simply stops
        being able to search IGDB until you set it up again.
      </p>
      <p>
        Technically minded: the two codes live in the <code>[igdb]</code> section
        of Studio's own settings file, <code>config.toml</code>, on your machine
        only.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="steps">
    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol class="guide-steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Sign in to Twitch's developer site</h3>
        <p>
          Open <a href="https://dev.twitch.tv/console/apps"
            >dev.twitch.tv/console/apps</a
          > and sign in with the Twitch account you want to use — or create one, it's
          free.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Turn on two-factor authentication</h3>
        <p>
          If your account doesn't already have it, Twitch will send you to turn
          it on first — look under Security in your account settings. Come back
          to the developer page once that's done.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Register an app</h3>
        <p>
          Click "Register Your Application." Give it any name — <code
            >Pito Studio</code
          > works fine, it's just a label. Where it asks for a redirect URL, type
          <code>http://localhost</code> — Studio doesn't actually use this field,
          but Twitch requires something in it. Pick any category; "Application Integration"
          is a reasonable choice.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Copy your two codes</h3>
        <p>
          Once the app exists, click "Manage." Your first code, the Client ID,
          is shown right away — copy it. Click "New Secret" to get the second
          one; Twitch only shows it to you once, so copy it somewhere safe
          before leaving the page.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Paste both into Studio</h3>
        <p>
          Back in Studio's setup wizard — or later, from Settings, next to IGDB
          — paste the first code, move to the next box with <span
            class="guide-key">Tab</span
          >, paste the second, then press <span class="guide-key">Enter</span> to
          save.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>Good to know</h2>
    <div class="guide-callout guide-callout--limits">
      <p>
        <strong>Two-factor authentication isn't optional.</strong> Every Twitch developer
        account needs it — that's Twitch's own rule, not something Pito adds.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The second code only shows once.</strong> Lose it, and you'll need
        to go back, make a new one, and update Studio.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Studio won't run into a limit.</strong> IGDB allows several requests
        every second, far more than a personal games library ever needs.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Your Twitch account itself is untouched.</strong> Registering this
        app doesn't affect your followers, your stream, or anything anyone else can
        see — it only exists so Studio can look things up.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="done">
    <h2>Back in Studio</h2>
    <div class="guide-callout guide-callout--done">
      <span class="guide-callout__tag">Last step</span>
      <p>
        With both codes saved, the Games screen can search and add games from
        IGDB right away — press <span class="guide-key">i</span> on Games, or let
        the setup wizard carry on to the next step. Nothing else to do.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
